WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.600
- Good morning Believe Nation
my name is Evan Carmichael,

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:05.389
my one word is believe and
I believe that entrepreneurs

00:00:05.389 --> 00:00:08.020
will solve all of the
worlds major problems.

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:10.079
So to help on your journey today's message

00:00:10.079 --> 00:00:12.162
is think outside the box.

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:14.870
Over to you Conor McGregor.

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:17.247
(rooster crows)

00:00:17.247 --> 00:00:19.588
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:19.588 --> 00:00:22.480
♫ Entspresso keep me going

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:26.317
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:26.317 --> 00:00:30.535
- Every gym I went to it was
always they had their set way.

00:00:30.535 --> 00:00:33.439
Every gym had their set
way of doing things.

00:00:33.439 --> 00:00:36.444
Don't do it this way, do it that way.

00:00:36.444 --> 00:00:39.419
So, that was just a normal
thing that I thought

00:00:39.419 --> 00:00:40.877
just people just had their own set way

00:00:40.877 --> 00:00:42.809
and you must do it that way.

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:44.816
You must stand this way,
you must kick this way,

00:00:44.816 --> 00:00:46.105
you must punch this way.

00:00:46.105 --> 00:00:47.884
But with John, when I went to John,

00:00:47.884 --> 00:00:49.933
and I met my coach John Kavanagh,

00:00:49.933 --> 00:00:52.358
at Straight Blast Gym,
it wasn't like that.

00:00:52.358 --> 00:00:55.631
He had a more open mind and he encouraged

00:00:55.631 --> 00:00:58.298
different movements and I
never experienced that before,

00:00:58.298 --> 00:01:01.154
I never heard a coach
say that to me before.

00:01:01.154 --> 00:01:02.608
And that stuck in my head
and I talked to myself,

00:01:02.608 --> 00:01:06.983
you know this guy is
thinking outside the box,

00:01:06.983 --> 00:01:08.347
you know what I mean, that he has a vision

00:01:08.347 --> 00:01:11.735
that everything works and
he encourages every movement

00:01:11.735 --> 00:01:13.838
because ultimately there is a time

00:01:13.838 --> 00:01:15.631
and a place for every single move.

00:01:15.631 --> 00:01:19.978
So I just knew when I met
John and when he spoke

00:01:19.978 --> 00:01:21.799
it made complete sense
to me and that was it,

00:01:21.799 --> 00:01:23.353
I stuck with him and he stuck with me

00:01:23.353 --> 00:01:24.955
and the rest is history.

00:01:24.955 --> 00:01:26.347
- So here's the easiest way I know of

00:01:26.347 --> 00:01:27.711
to think outside the box.

00:01:27.711 --> 00:01:28.625
A lot of people think you have to be

00:01:28.625 --> 00:01:31.046
a creative genius, you
have to be artistic,

00:01:31.046 --> 00:01:32.891
you have to think a certain way

00:01:32.891 --> 00:01:34.738
to think outside the box, you don't.

00:01:34.738 --> 00:01:37.241
Anybody can think outside the box.

00:01:37.241 --> 00:01:39.278
For me, the easiest way to do it

00:01:39.278 --> 00:01:42.206
is just take something
from another industry

00:01:42.206 --> 00:01:45.574
that you also love and
bring it to your market.

00:01:45.574 --> 00:01:48.331
This is where if you combine
the genius of the aim,

00:01:48.331 --> 00:01:51.322
if you embrace the
things that make you you,

00:01:51.322 --> 00:01:53.004
that's where you win
that nobody else can win

00:01:53.004 --> 00:01:56.040
because you have all sorts
of different interests.

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:57.657
That if you narrow down and you only pick

00:01:57.657 --> 00:01:59.521
one thing that you're going to focus on,

00:01:59.521 --> 00:02:01.030
this is the only thing you're going to do,

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:02.548
one you're going to feel left out

00:02:02.548 --> 00:02:03.729
because there's all these other interests

00:02:03.729 --> 00:02:06.428
that you have and two
you're going to get stopped

00:02:06.428 --> 00:02:09.018
because the people who love
that thing more than you

00:02:09.018 --> 00:02:10.981
are going to go crush you,
where if you can combine

00:02:10.981 --> 00:02:14.776
all these things down
into one, you embrace

00:02:14.776 --> 00:02:16.469
everything that you love and find a way

00:02:16.469 --> 00:02:19.783
to mix it up into a pot,
people can't replicate you.

00:02:19.783 --> 00:02:21.721
You're unique, you have a unique voice,

00:02:21.721 --> 00:02:25.251
you have a unique approach,
people can't just copy you.

00:02:25.251 --> 00:02:28.081
The best they're ever going to
be is a junior version of you.

00:02:28.081 --> 00:02:30.111
And so you think outside the box

00:02:30.111 --> 00:02:31.951
by taking all these different interests

00:02:31.951 --> 00:02:33.949
and taking something that's
worked in something else

00:02:33.949 --> 00:02:35.692
and bring it to the thing you're doing

00:02:35.692 --> 00:02:37.141
and people now will suddenly say that

00:02:37.141 --> 00:02:39.563
you are a creative genius,
that you're so great

00:02:39.563 --> 00:02:41.896
thinking outside the box,
but all you're really doing

00:02:41.896 --> 00:02:43.594
is just pulling from different industries.

00:02:43.594 --> 00:02:44.827
And this worked over and over and

00:02:44.827 --> 00:02:46.554
over and over and over again.

00:02:46.554 --> 00:02:49.060
I looked at Starbucks,
I love Howard Schultz,

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:53.718
he's on the wall in my office,
he got the idea for Starbucks

00:02:53.718 --> 00:02:55.605
when he went on a trip to Milan.

00:02:55.605 --> 00:02:57.343
He went to Italy and he
saw that the coffee shops

00:02:57.343 --> 00:03:01.042
were the center of life in
Italy, and had been for decades.

00:03:01.042 --> 00:03:02.918
That was the third home,
they had their office,

00:03:02.918 --> 00:03:05.278
they had their home and then
they had the coffee shop

00:03:05.278 --> 00:03:07.396
where people would hang
out, be a meeting place.

00:03:07.396 --> 00:03:08.656
And he looked at North America

00:03:08.656 --> 00:03:10.253
and he saw the Dunkin'
Donuts of the world,

00:03:10.253 --> 00:03:12.224
where you just come in
and out, grab a coffee.

00:03:12.224 --> 00:03:14.430
It's more utility and he wanted to create

00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:17.114
that same third home
vibe in North America.

00:03:17.114 --> 00:03:19.230
It worked in Italy for decades,

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:22.871
let's just bring it to
North America and boom,

00:03:22.871 --> 00:03:26.719
one of the biggest brains
in the world was born.

00:03:26.719 --> 00:03:28.889
Not because Howard's
necessarily a creative genius,

00:03:28.889 --> 00:03:31.764
but because he borrowed
from other industries

00:03:31.764 --> 00:03:32.847
and brought it to his own.

00:03:32.847 --> 00:03:34.341
You look at this YouTube channel.

00:03:34.341 --> 00:03:35.977
There's a couple things
that I brought here

00:03:35.977 --> 00:03:39.571
from other industries that
have helped my channel grow.

00:03:39.571 --> 00:03:42.298
I have a salsa business, I was a salsa DJ.

00:03:42.298 --> 00:03:44.943
Part of DJing is taking
lots of different songs

00:03:44.943 --> 00:03:46.549
and mixing them up in a way that

00:03:46.549 --> 00:03:48.333
makes sense for a dance party.

00:03:48.333 --> 00:03:50.364
I do that for a lot of my top 10's

00:03:50.364 --> 00:03:51.776
and for a lot of the videos that I do.

00:03:51.776 --> 00:03:54.463
I pull together different
clips that I feel

00:03:54.463 --> 00:03:55.943
work well together and we make it

00:03:55.943 --> 00:03:58.930
into an experience for you guys to enjoy.

00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:01.108
Nobody else was doing that and definitely

00:04:01.108 --> 00:04:04.209
in a business context, an
entrepreneurship context

00:04:04.209 --> 00:04:06.660
on YouTube and it's helped me win.

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:08.373
I also approached YouTube as a business,

00:04:08.373 --> 00:04:10.127
I'm an entrepreneur.

00:04:10.127 --> 00:04:12.468
The idea that I want
to respond to everybody

00:04:12.468 --> 00:04:14.445
who leaves a comment and I've hired people

00:04:14.445 --> 00:04:16.745
on my team and might need
to hire somebody else

00:04:16.745 --> 00:04:18.639
'cause you guys are commenting so quickly

00:04:18.639 --> 00:04:20.827
that we're falling behind a little bit.

00:04:20.827 --> 00:04:23.969
I want everybody to have
a response on a comment.

00:04:23.969 --> 00:04:26.111
When somebody leaves a comment,
I want to respond to them.

00:04:26.111 --> 00:04:27.758
I think it's important, I think
it's just part of business.

00:04:27.758 --> 00:04:29.839
If somebody calls your store or walks

00:04:29.839 --> 00:04:31.827
into your front door, you're
going to talk to them,

00:04:31.827 --> 00:04:34.182
you're going to thank them,
you're going to acknowledge them.

00:04:34.182 --> 00:04:36.810
And at the beginning of
a lot of YouTube channels

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:39.126
creators will respond to every comment,

00:04:39.126 --> 00:04:41.028
they say I respond to every comment,

00:04:41.028 --> 00:04:43.134
and it's easy because you
have one comment a day

00:04:43.134 --> 00:04:44.789
or ten comments a day, it's not that hard

00:04:44.789 --> 00:04:46.466
to respond to every comment.

00:04:46.466 --> 00:04:48.251
But when you're getting two, three, 400

00:04:48.251 --> 00:04:51.922
comments a day, let's
see if you can keep up.

00:04:51.922 --> 00:04:55.029
And so they can't and
so they just drop it.

00:04:55.029 --> 00:04:57.008
Where my approach is no,
you don't just drop it,

00:04:57.008 --> 00:05:00.398
it's still important, you just
bring on people to help you.

00:05:00.398 --> 00:05:02.240
You don't have to do
everything in your business.

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:05.259
Just like in the real business world,

00:05:05.259 --> 00:05:06.480
I keep knocking my books over.

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:10.380
When you go in the real business world,

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:12.911
you start off doing everything yourself

00:05:12.911 --> 00:05:14.865
and then you bring on a team to help you

00:05:14.865 --> 00:05:16.572
and you move up and you
start focusing on things

00:05:16.572 --> 00:05:18.788
that you're really, really,
really, really good at.

00:05:18.788 --> 00:05:20.816
And so taking that
approach will bring you to

00:05:20.816 --> 00:05:22.414
my YouTube channel,
where still most creators

00:05:22.414 --> 00:05:26.993
of my size are not responding
to most of the comments.

00:05:26.993 --> 00:05:29.118
Right, they may respond to a couple,

00:05:29.118 --> 00:05:30.398
and then they just leave it,

00:05:30.398 --> 00:05:32.683
where I love Believe Nation,

00:05:32.683 --> 00:05:35.099
I love to communicate,
I thinks it's important

00:05:35.099 --> 00:05:37.000
that you guys are
connecting and getting along

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:39.566
and there's a vibrant discussion.

00:05:39.566 --> 00:05:41.952
And so I want to make
sure there's recognition

00:05:41.952 --> 00:05:44.483
and acknowledgement so I
hire people to help me.

00:05:44.483 --> 00:05:47.009
And so I talked to the guys at YouTube

00:05:47.009 --> 00:05:48.557
with my account manager and he said wow,

00:05:48.557 --> 00:05:50.832
that's such an outside the box idea.

00:05:50.832 --> 00:05:52.697
It's not that outside the box,

00:05:52.697 --> 00:05:54.459
it's not that creative it's just taking

00:05:54.459 --> 00:05:57.700
something that works in business
and bringing it to YouTube.

00:05:57.700 --> 00:06:00.634
And so you don't need to be
an insanely creative person.

00:06:00.634 --> 00:06:03.288
You don't need to be the wild character

00:06:03.288 --> 00:06:05.403
who's always thinking outside the box.

00:06:05.403 --> 00:06:07.462
The way to do it is to take other things

00:06:07.462 --> 00:06:09.840
that you're passionate
about, if you love baseball,

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:12.689
or you love Celine Dion
or you love beavers

00:06:12.689 --> 00:06:15.612
or you love grocery
stores or you love cars,

00:06:15.612 --> 00:06:19.400
whatever it is, your various
interests, you love the smurfs.

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:22.953
You know whatever it
is, find out something

00:06:22.953 --> 00:06:27.012
that works there in that
community, in that business model

00:06:27.012 --> 00:06:29.943
and just bring it to what
you're currently doing.

00:06:29.943 --> 00:06:32.955
And if it's different and
it makes you stand out,

00:06:32.955 --> 00:06:36.177
people will suddenly tout you as the next

00:06:36.177 --> 00:06:39.472
creative genius and
outside the box thinker.

00:06:39.472 --> 00:06:40.698
So the question today is I'm curious

00:06:40.698 --> 00:06:43.852
what is one of the more
outside the box ideas

00:06:43.852 --> 00:06:45.046
that you've had, that you've pulled from

00:06:45.046 --> 00:06:47.770
a different industry and
applied to your business?

00:06:47.770 --> 00:06:48.759
Really curious to find out,

00:06:48.759 --> 00:06:50.135
leave it down in the comments below.

00:06:50.135 --> 00:06:52.550
I also want to give a
quick shout to Gary Wilson.

00:06:52.550 --> 00:06:54.455
Gary thank you so much
for picking up a copy

00:06:54.455 --> 00:06:56.731
of my book, Your One
Word, I really appreciate

00:06:56.731 --> 00:06:59.545
the support Gary and I
hope you enjoyed the read.

00:06:59.545 --> 00:07:01.096
So thank you guys so much for watching,

00:07:01.096 --> 00:07:03.397
I believe in you, I hope
you continue to believe

00:07:03.397 --> 00:07:06.292
in yourself and whatever your one word is

00:07:06.292 --> 00:07:08.804
much love and I'll see
you again tomorrow morning

00:07:08.804 --> 00:07:11.956
for another shot of Entspresso.

00:07:11.956 --> 00:07:14.409
♫ I wake up every morning

00:07:14.409 --> 00:07:18.249
♫ Entspresso keep me going

00:07:18.249 --> 00:07:21.253
(typewriter sound)

00:07:21.253 --> 00:07:24.600
- My thinking is so
superior and my knowledge

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:27.985
is so positive and my logic is so wise

00:07:27.985 --> 00:07:31.596
until it clashes with the mentality

00:07:31.596 --> 00:07:34.082
which is down here and I'm up there.

00:07:34.082 --> 00:07:34.915
